From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_data_plane: do not skip open after a close that failed

The data plane keeps a per-interface notify_open shadow so that a
repeated open or close costs no firmware round trip. Open is skipped
when the shadow says the firmware interface is already open, and close
is skipped when it says it is already closed.

The shadow is only updated when the message succeeds. A close that fails
therefore leaves it reading "open" - and the very next open returns
NSS_DP_SUCCESS without sending anything to the firmware at all.

That is the wrong way round for this message pair. nss_phys_if_close()
is synchronous: it sends NSS_PHYS_IF_CLOSE and then waits three seconds
for the reply. When it reports failure the usual reason is that the
reply did not arrive in time - the message itself was already handed to
the firmware, and the firmware very probably did close the interface.
So the one case where the shadow is most likely to be wrong is exactly
the case where it is left untouched, and it is left claiming the state
that suppresses the message which would repair it.

The consequence is a physical port that never transmits again. Its
firmware interface is shut while the host believes it is open, so the
host and the firmware both keep accepting frames for it and none reach
the wire: carrier up, the MAC's receive counters climbing and its
transmit counters at exactly zero, the firmware's per-port egress
counter frozen, and no error reported by anything. A link cycle does not
touch it, because a link-state notification is not an open. Tearing the
data plane down and bringing it back does not touch it either, because
the open on the way back is the one being skipped. Only a reboot clears
it, since nothing else re-boots the firmware or reloads the driver.

Clear the shadow whichever way the close goes. A close that failed
leaves the firmware's state unknown, and the safe reading of unknown is
"assume it is closed": an open sent to an interface that is already open
costs one message, while an open withheld from one that is closed costs
the port. This is the same discipline the glue already applies to link
state, where a notification the firmware did not acknowledge obliges the
next one to be sent rather than skipped.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
--- a/nss_data_plane/nss_data_plane.c
+++ b/nss_data_plane/nss_data_plane.c
@@ -65,8 +65,21 @@ static int __nss_data_plane_close(struct
 		return NSS_DP_SUCCESS;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Drop the shadow before the message rather than after it succeeds.
+	 * nss_phys_if_close() waits for the firmware's reply and reports
+	 * failure when it times out, by which point the close itself has
+	 * already been sent - so a failure here means the interface's state is
+	 * unknown, not that it is still open. Leaving the shadow set would let
+	 * the next open short-circuit and send nothing, and a firmware
+	 * interface left shut under a host that believes it is open never
+	 * transmits again until the box is rebooted. An open sent to an
+	 * already-open interface costs one message; an open withheld from a
+	 * closed one costs the port.
+	 */
+	dp->notify_open = 0;
+
 	if (nss_phys_if_close(dp->nss_ctx, dp->if_num) == NSS_TX_SUCCESS) {
-		dp->notify_open = 0;
 		return NSS_DP_SUCCESS;
 	}
 	return NSS_DP_FAILURE;
